\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Duals and the identity map}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The star on the field pullback #{a^*} is the dual map, and #{V^*}
is the dual module. Mathematical physics also uses the star for an
adjoint and for complex conjugation. Geometric algebra writes #{A^*}
for the dual #{A{\psI}^{-1}}. Here the star is only the dual. That
geometric dual is the product with #{\psI^{-1}}, and the adjoint is
#{A^\dagger}.}

\p{The identity map is #{\bbI}, written #{\bbI_M} on a named module.
Mathematical physics writes #{\mathbb 1}, #{I}, or #{\operatorname{id}},
and geometric calculus writes #{1} for the identity function. The unit
of a ring or of an algebra stays #{1}: it is an element, not a map.
The pseudoscalar #{\psI} is a different mark.}
